  "theory": {
    "intro": "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is $1$. The unit vector pointing the same way as $\\underset{\\sim}{a}$ is written $\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}$, and it is found by dividing $\\underset{\\sim}{a}$ by its own magnitude.\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\\large}, scale=1.2]\n\\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}\n\\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(4,2.4) node[right]{$\\underset{\\sim}{a}$};\n\\draw[-{Stealth},thick,blue] (0,0)--(1.286,0.771) node[midway,above left]{$\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]",
    "facts": [
      "$\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}=\\frac{1}{|\\underset{\\sim}{a}|}\\underset{\\sim}{a}$, so every component is divided by the magnitude.",
      "$\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}$ points the same way as $\\underset{\\sim}{a}$ and has magnitude exactly $1$.",
      "The unit vector in the opposite direction to $\\underset{\\sim}{a}$ is $-\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}$.",
      "Read backwards, $\\underset{\\sim}{a}=|\\underset{\\sim}{a}|\\,\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}$: a vector is its length times its direction.",
      "A vector of magnitude $r$ in the direction of $\\underset{\\sim}{a}$ is $r\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}$.",
      "Leave the answer exact, such as $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(3\\underset{\\sim}{i}+2\\underset{\\sim}{j})$, unless a decimal is asked for.",
      "Check any unit vector by squaring its components: they must add to $1$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Find the magnitude $|\\underset{\\sim}{a}|$.",
      "Divide the vector by its magnitude, giving $\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}=\\frac{1}{|\\underset{\\sim}{a}|}\\underset{\\sim}{a}$.",
      "Check that the components of $\\hat{\\underset{\\sim}{a}}$ square and add to $1$."
    ]
  },
